course: dictionary
lesson: 9837
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: One word can have several meanings, and the sentence tells you which one to use. The word swimming is a good example. «The ducks are swimming on the pond» means they are moving through the water right now. «Sam goes swimming every Saturday» means he does the sport or activity.
explain: Look at the words next to it. «A swimming pool» tells you swimming is used before a noun, to describe what the pool is for. «My head is swimming» is not about water at all: it means you feel dizzy.
explain: Ask yourself: which meaning makes sense here? Then put your meaning into the sentence in place of the word. If it still makes sense, you have probably chosen well.
fact: Swimming is the -ing form of the verb swim, so it can show an action that is happening now.
fact: «Swimming» can be the name of a sport or activity, as in «go swimming» or «swimming lessons».
fact: Before a noun, swimming describes a thing meant for swimming, such as «a swimming costume» or «swimming trunks».
fact: «My head is swimming» means you feel dizzy, or your eyes are full of tears.
fact: Swimming is an activity that needs safety: swim between the flags and never swim alone.
exercise: «The ducks are swimming on the pond.» What does swimming mean here? | A swimming pool for children | Feeling dizzy | The sport of swimming in a pool | *Moving through water right now
exercise: «The penguins are built for swimming.» What kind of word helps describe what the penguins are built for? | A word that means very fast running | *A word meaning a thing made for swimming | A word that means the name of a town | A word that means very quiet
exercise: «After spinning round, my head is swimming.» What does this mean? | I have had a bath | I am swimming in my head | *I feel dizzy | Water is pouring out of my ears
test: «Learn deep-water swimming step by step with a teacher, and only swim where an adult is watching.» What does this sentence tell you to do? | Wait until you are a grown-up | Swim alone in the deep water to learn quickly | Watch others swim and never try yourself | *Learn with a teacher and swim only where an adult is watching
test: «The moment they dive, penguins shoot through the water like torpedoes, because their bodies are built for swimming.» What is swimming describing here? | *Moving through the water, the way a penguin's body is made to do | A swimming pool where penguins live | Feeling very tired after a long day | Sitting still on the ice
exam: Nadia says swimming always means the sport. Is she right? | No, it means a type of fish | Yes, but only in the water | *No, it can also mean moving through water right now, or a thing made for swimming | Yes, swimming only ever means the sport
